 diverse in multiple  locations  and it makes it harder and harder to  roll out changes that affect the entire  environment  so the way we deal with that in  anthoconfig management  is through a tool called config sync  that puts all of your configuration in a  centralized  policy repository that's based on git  that means everybody in the operating  team can collaborate together  using methods that they typically use  for application development  pull requests and code reviews and that  helps your team work better together  and then by connecting that  multi-platform environment back to that  central repository  it helps your environment work better by  having a single  centralized source of truth for all of  your configuration  config management stores that  configuration as a secure repository and  then rolls it out to all of your  kubernetes clusters  and to antho service mesh and that means  all of your configuration is auditable  that there's a record of every change  that's made to the environment  stored in that git repository it's  revertable  in terms of being able to roll back the  last  best good state uh it's transactional  and that changes can be grouped together  and rolled out all at once  and it's self-healing that single source  of truth  is kept in sync with all of your  clusters  and any local changes are guarded  against drift so that you can be assured  that it is applied  how that looks in a production  environment might be something like this  so if you have google cloud platform  with a gke cluster running on the left  and an on-prem data center that's  running gk and prem on the right  you can connect those through cloud  interconnect and have a single  common private network the policy  repository that holds all of your  configuration  can live on-prem or it can live in gcp  in this case  it's running on-prem and that policy  repository allows you to  define your configuration using  abstract namespaces or inheriting  configuration across  multiple teams so you can define  policies that apply globally  in addition to that you can specify  configuration  should be rolled out to a single cluster  or a set of clusters when you need to  configure things differently  now let's talk about managing policies  at scale with policy controller  policy controller is included in  anthoconfig management and it is based  on the open policy agent  gatekeeper project in open source it  allows you to manage  policy at multiple stages of config  deployment  and so you can inspect configuration  before it gets applied  you can enforce policies at the api  layer inspecting every call that comes  into your kubernetes environment  and then you can run audits using the  same policies to look at  historical changes or simply just to  inspect things without necessarily  enforcing them actively  and that means that any client that is  connected to your  clusters is respecting  the guardrails that you have in place  that enact your security and compliance  policies  those policies are diverse and that's  why we support custom  policies in policy controller the policy  controller also comes with a default  policy library  that includes 27 constraint templates  that  are used for common use cases that we  see all the time with our customers  and these are policies that are  maintained by google and  included as part of the distribution  that might be something like  restricting a role binding to only your  corporate domain  or ensuring that mutual tls is turned on  so that all of the traffic running over  your service match  is encrypted in transit at all times  finally let's talk about managing gcp  resources  as part of memphis  gcp config connector is designed to  expose all of the popular gcp services  that our customers use as part of  kubernetes  and anthos it does that by  taking every popular gcp api  and turning it into a kubernetes api so  that when you're deploying applications  through cubecontrol  through a git based deployment like  config management or through api clients  you can  treat gcpus that were just another part  of that  kubernetes environment that means you've  got native  native api service for gcp and  you can specify the resources that you  would like that underlie your  applications whether that's a database  or storage bucket in declarative  configuration  and that configuration will be  continuously reconciled against the  cloud  and made guarded against drift  this is available now and it is included  as part of antos config manager so  all of these components can be installed  separately but they can all be used  together  and that means you can store all of your  configuration whether it's kubernetes  service mesh or gcp in a single common  git repository  roll that out and check it for policies  that ensure that it's compliant with  your  security rules and your operational  controls  and extend that way of managing  configuration  from kubernetes all the way to gcp and  that's santos config management  that concludes part one of our deep dive  stay tuned for part two where we'll talk  about cloud render and those  and anthony service members on behalf of  vic  and myself and all of google cloud  thanks for joining us today

