 control plane that allows you to  create  many user clusters and those user  clusters are the ones that we're going  to  hand over to the end users of kubernetes  this admin cluster makes sure that the  right number of nodes is provisioned  that the right plugins are installed  into into your clusters  that the right functionality is  available to your end users  and we'll make sure that that state  stays up to date so once you have that  admin cluster  that control plane up and running you  can start to create user clusters  and that is done by creating a  kubernetes  resource and applying that into the  admin cluster  once you do that the admin cluster will  look at the configuration that you've  asked for as far as what that cluster  should have in it  the size should be how many nodes it  should have and it will go ahead  and provision that user cluster for you  and then you can get some credentials  out of there  and hand them back to your users this  can  create up to 10 user clusters per  uh gk on on-prem installation and each  of those  10 user clusters can have 100 nodes this  really allows you to scale  to a large number of kubernetes clusters  within each  data center footprint and then provide  those to various use cases within your  organization  once you have those user clusters and  the master  the control plane up and running you'll  also want to include load balancing  and load balancing gets traffic into  your workloads  so the types of load balancers that we  have available today are one  inbuilt load balancer which is what we  recommend this is based on  uh some google technology that we  provide l3 load balancing on  on your on behalf of the clusters you  could also bring  an f5 uh big-ip if that was how you were  doing  uh the load balancing in your  organization or you could bring  your own load balancer that was not  fully integrated by us  and configure that to point at your user  clusters and your  uh admin control plane a lot of  flexibility there  to get the right setup that you need  on-prem is one of the places where we  think about clusters being uh  required but we also know that you have  other clouds that you've been uh  committed to and have workloads running  in so we want to make sure  that you can run anthos gke on any of  the other cloud providers  the next thing we'll talk about is  anthon's gke on aws  there's a lot of benefits to having this  gke on aws  uh one is you get all the functionality  that i talked about in the earlier uh  part of the talk where we're managing  clusters for you we're integrating  monitoring  those pieces and really it allows you to  integrate  those that functionality into your  existing aws footprint  so super useful stuff the pattern with  anthoc gke on aws is  that you'll have a jump host similar to  how we had  the uh the user laptop in in the ethos  gk on-prem example  and that jump host will have a binary on  it called enthos gke  and that binary will talk to the ec2 api  and provision again an admin cluster  which will act as your control plane  for other clusters that then you provide  to your users so once you've provisioned  that admin cluster  you're going to go ahead and run  commands against it  to provision your user clusters again  the same pattern that we had before  what we do get as a benefit by running  inside of aws  is we have an inbuilt load balancer in  elastic load balancing  elb that comes out of the box so you  don't have to  configure or run your own load balancer  in that case  as far as features that are available in  anthos gke on aws  you have the ability to have h8 control  planes auto scaling  in place upgrades and your storage  backed by aws's ebs  if you don't have an existing vpc and  want to just kick the tires you could  absolutely  create your own vpc through the enthos  gk on aws installer  or you can choose to install into an  existing vpc  and all of these clusters that you bring  up are going to be well integrated with  anthoconfig management  and those service management and cloud  run on anthos  which we'll talk about in part two of  this talk  so now that we have clusters all over  the place  how are we gonna manage all the  workloads across them how we're gonna  make sure that they are  as uniform as possible that we know  what's running well  you know we're gonna have uh some  challenges there and  anthos is willing to help you here so  we know that users have a lot of  different multi-cluster use cases  we talked about some around data  locality being near to databases but you  also might want to have highly available  services  you might have different environments  that have different clusters  and you might have for example a low  latency service that you want to have  with clusters that have are in different  regions  so the first thing that you might want  to do since if you're already a  kubernetes user is attach a cluster to  anthos that you've already provisioned  somewhere  through some other mechanism and we  allow that so you can bring your own  clusters to anthos  no matter how they are provisioned as  long as they're compliant  kubernetes clusters the way that you do  that  is you would register them through the  google cloud console or via the cli  and that would attach them to our to our  anthos control plane  the way that that actually works is that  we have a agent that runs inside of your  cluster  and that calls out of your cluster and  connects to our control plane in a  secure manner  it actually encrypts that connection  that's egressing  your environment and make sure that it's  secure  and then with that connection nailed up  we can start to do  a higher order things like provide low  balancing across clusters  or just show you your workloads running  for those clusters inside of the  google cloud console  another thing that you get out of  attaching these clusters is you get to  start  turning on and off features that you  might want to have using enthos so for  example you might want to turn on  anthon's config management which we'll  talk about uh in the last last half of  this talk  you might want to set up load balancing  across the clusters and you can do all  of that  through the the anthos ui  once they're attached they're going to  show up just like any other gke on gcp  cluster  so you'll have those listed here you'll  be able to see which ones should be  upgraded  what the capacity of those clusters is  and whether they're healthy  so super useful stuff so we can talk  about  multi-cluster ingress multi-cluster  ingress  allows us to have a single load balancer  ip  that can point to multiple clusters  within our anthos deployment  so now i can route people based on the  lowest latency cluster  that would reach them and if i have a  failover scenario  i can route to an additional cluster  that may be in a different region or a  different zone  or maybe in a data center so with these  features we're really trying to make  sure  that anthos has the ability to bring to  you  higher complex uh use cases in a really  simple way that maps to how we do things  in the kubernetes world  and with that we're going to go ahead  and take a look at how these things fit  together  through the cloud console and we're  going to look at how we manage our  clusters  from gke on gcp gk on aws  and gk on-prem  all right since we now seen how anthos  can get us clusters provisioned  and we've seen some of the methodologies  for how that's done let's take a look at  what we end up with  when we put all of these things together  and we'll explore this using the gcp  console  so here we are on the homepage i'm going  to go ahead and click on  the hamburger menu over here and then  down to anthos  the end those dashboard is meant to tell  you your cluster status what features  you have enabled on each of those  clusters  we can see here that we have six  clusters currently  in anthous management and we'll see here  that  some of them are provided by vsphere so  this is our gk on-prem cluster  we also have some in u.s central within  uh gcp  us east within gcp then also one  registered  with enthus from aws you can see all  these clusters show up  in the same place and we can also check  on  the current status of their  configurations to make sure that they're  synced  to the get ops configuration that we've  put into our repository  we can see that all from the anthos  dashboard here  if we go into the kubernetes dashboard  we can actually see some  deeper insights into what's running in  our clusters at this at this time  so here we have the list of all of our  clusters we can see that we have  clusters again  in gcp aws and vsphere and they all show  up  in the same pane class here we're going  to go over and look at our workloads  so this can be used as a ui for  kubernetes  so here i have a saved faceted view  for my dope app this is an application  that's running across a bunch of  different clusters so we can  see here that i have some running inside  of  my production clusters some in my  staging clusters  and some on my dev clusters on prem so  you can see all of these in a single  pane of glass and when i click into  these workloads i can actually  start to see some of the monitoring and  metrics of how they're operating so you  can have this  as a developer portal as well so they  can help debug i can also see  the events that might have been fired  for this particular  workload and i can go ahead and look at  my services  that were provisioned in here so a lot  of functionality that can be found  for any of your clusters that are  registered with nthos  the other thing that you might want to  do is check out your logs and have an  integrating logging integrated logging  view  so with that we have the logs view  where you can filter on a bunch of  different things including  the container type the cluster or a  particular kubernetes node if that's  giving you trouble  so if we click on kubernetes cluster we  can see these are the  logs from our clusters that are  registered in gcp  and we can see all of the information  about the resources that have been  applied  we can also go ahead and click into a  particular container workload  and see the faceted view of where those  are running so here we can see  the clusters i can click on for example  my on-prem  dev cluster and i can see all the logs  for my workloads running there  i can then filter that down for example  by  namespace so i might click into the cube  system namespace because something isn't  quite running right  and i can start to see all of the  information about things that are going  wrong there  next i wanted to show how i might add a  namespace  to all of my clusters since i have the  ability to manage these clusters in a  uniform way  how how might i add a particular  namespace  all right so here we have git repository  that contains all the configuration that  we want shared across  our clusters so for example if i wanted  to add a namespace  i could go to the namespaces folder and  add a file in here  that has some kubernetes manifest that  i'd want added so i'm going to go ahead  and add a new file here  and i'm gonna call it namespaces slash  my awesome  and in that i'm gonna create a  namespace.yaml  i'm actually gonna overload this a  little bit and i'm gonna also include  not just a namespace definition but  actually a pod and so we'll see my  namespace get created  which is going to be called my awesome  app  and we're going to have a pod created  which is going to be this web server pod  so we should see when we go back to the  console that these are all showing up in  our clusters so let's go ahead and  commit these  now when you commit these these changes  they can be reviewed by folks  you can be you can have a transaction  log of all these changes that have gone  through  just like any other git repo so let's go  take a look at what this looks like  inside of our console so i go back to my  kubernetes engine clusters here  and now i should be able to see and  filter on  my namespace that i just created and see  those pods coming up with that web  server  enabled so let's see here okay  i see that i have all my clusters here i  go to the workloads pane which is going  to contain all the workloads in all my  clusters  and i'm going to go ahead and reset this  one which was for my previous  application i was using i'm going to  look for my new app called my  awesome app great  so we can see here we've already got  some of these coming up so we have a  prod west cluster  that's running our pod and our dev  on-prem cluster  so that's it we've got things running  and now we'll hand it off to john  who's going to take us through a little  bit more in detail what acm  anthous config management can provide  thanks fake  i'm going to talk a little bit about  anthos config management  ethos config management helps you manage  all of your configuration policies at  scale  and includes three different components  there's config sync  for managing configuration policy  controller for managing your governance  and compliance  policies and config connector for  bringing gcp  into atos first let's talk about  config sync and how it helps you manage  configuration and scale  so when you've got a large and diverse  hybrid  environment that runs on kubernetes you  have a couple of scale problems  one is scaling your team you've got many  people on the operator team  who need to apply configuration changes  to that environment  whether it's on gcp on-prem or in other  public clouds  and as that team grows making changes  against that environment by running  commands through cute control becomes  harder and harder to keep straight  you don't know who's doing what and you  don't necessarily have a record of  things that have changed  on the flip side all of those clusters  and all of the different teams sharing  them  get bigger and more diverse in multiple  locations  and it makes it harder and harder to  roll out changes that affect the entire  environment  so the way we deal with that in  anthoconfig management  is through a tool called config sync  that puts all of your configuration in a  centralized  policy repository that's based on git  that means everybody in the operating  team can collaborate together  using methods that they typically use  for application development  pull requests and code reviews and that  helps your team work better together  and then by connecting that  multi-platform environment back to that  central repository  it helps your environment work better by  having a single  centralized source of truth for all of  your configuration  config management stores that  configuration as a secure repository and  then rolls it out to all of your  kubernetes clusters  and to antho service mesh and that means  all of your configuration is auditable  that there's a record of every change  that's made to the environment  stored in that git repository it's  revertable  in terms of being able to roll back the  last  best good state uh it's transactional  and that changes can be grouped together  and rolled out all at once  and it's self-healing that single source  of truth  is kept in sync with all of your  clusters  and any local changes are guarded  against drift so that you can be assured  that it is applied  how that looks in a production  environment might be something like this  so if you have google cloud platform  with a gke cluster running on the left  and an on-prem data center that's  running gk and prem on the right  you can connect those through cloud  interconnect and have a single  common private network the policy  repository that holds all of your  configuration  can live on-prem or it can live in gcp  in this case  it's running on-prem and that policy  repository allows you to  define your configuration using  abstract namespaces or inheriting  configuration across  multiple teams so you can define  policies that apply globally  in addition to that you can specify  configuration  should be rolled out to a single cluster  or a set of clusters when you need to  configure things differently  now let's talk about managing policies  at scale with policy controller  policy controller is included in  anthoconfig management and it is based  on the open policy agent  gatekeeper project in open source it  allows you to manage  policy at multiple stages of config  deployment  and so you can inspect configuration  before it gets applied  you can enforce policies at the api  layer inspecting every call that comes  into your kubernetes environment  and then you can run audits using the  same policies to look at  historical changes or simply just to  inspect things without necessarily  enforcing them actively  and that means that any client that is  connected to your  clusters is respecting  the guardrails that you have in place  that enact your security and compliance  policies  those policies are diverse and that's  why we support custom  policies in policy controller the policy  controller also comes with a default  policy library  that includes 27 constraint templates  that  are used for common use cases that we  see all the time with our customers  and these are policies that are  maintained by google and  included as part of the distribution  that might be something like  restricting a role binding to only your  corporate domain  or ensuring that mutual tls is turned on  so that all of the traffic running over  your service match  is encrypted in transit at all times  finally let's talk about managing gcp  resources  as part of memphis  gcp config connector is designed to  expose all of the popular gcp services  that our customers use as part of  kubernetes  and anthos it does that by  taking every popular gcp api  and turning it into a kubernetes api so  that when you're deploying applications  through cubecontrol  through a git based deployment like  config management or through api clients  you can  treat gcpus that were just another part  of that  kubernetes environment that means you've  got native  native api service for gcp and  you can specify the resources that you  would like that underlie your  applications whether that's a database  or storage bucket in declarative  configuration  and that configuration will be  continuously reconciled against the  cloud  and made guarded against drift  this is available now and it is included  as part of antos config manager so  all of these components can be installed  separately but they can all be used  together  and that means you can store all of your  configuration whether it's kubernetes  service mesh or gcp in a single common  git repository  roll that out and check it for policies  that ensure that it's compliant with  your  security rules and your operational  controls  and extend that way of managing  configuration  from kubernetes all the way to gcp and  that's santos config management  that concludes part one of our deep dive  stay tuned for part two where we'll talk  about cloud render and those  and anthony service members on behalf of  vic  and myself and all of google cloud  thanks for joining us today
 

